+### Tetrahedral Polygonal Approximation
+Transforms all volume data into tetrahedra pieces, where each piece is allocated transparency and color values based on position and distance from the viewer, then scan converts these pieces using a painters algorithm [S2, unofficial]. Choose when a simple, non-differentiable rasterization pipeline is sufficient for static volume visualization. [S2, unofficial]
 
 ## Background
-The concept of piecewise-linear topology and simplicial complexes forms the mathematical foundation for unstructured meshes [G]. Simplicial complexes are the principal building blocks, comprising simplices (vertices, edges, triangles, tetrahedra) and cells [G]. The most general representation, the unstructured mesh, explicitly specifies both geometry and micro-topology using $n$-tuples of indices into a vertex list to form $n$-cells [S1, unofficial]. While structured meshes simplify compression by requiring only geometric information, unstructured meshes are necessary for problems not defined on regular grids, such as those in computational fluid dynamics (CFD) and finite element analysis (FEA) [S1, unofficial]. Memory consumption for an unstructured mesh with 32-bit floats for vertices and 32-bit integers for cells is approximately 32 bits/vertex, $3 \log_2(v)$ bits/triangle for triangle incidence, and $4 \log_2(v)$ bits/tetrahedron for tetrahedron incidence, where $v$ is the total number of vertices [S1, unofficial]. Early rendering approaches using tetrahedral meshes involved transforming volume data into tetrahedra pieces and scan converting them using a painters algorithm [S2, unofficial].
